You stand at the foot of the ramp with the dock's one pump rattling somewhere below, and you are thinking about a thing you want badly and have told nobody. It is a small, stubborn dream, of the kind that comes out when there is nothing to hit. Across the apron Vance Oduya works his berth console, glancing at you over its edge now and then, plainly unsure whether you are waiting for something. You are. You are waiting for the dream to finish its sentence. Beside the ramp rail, a line of water-ration notices flaps in the vent draft, and you notice, not for the first time, that they have been peeling in exactly the same direction since you arrived.

